Overlay patch 0043: route OoT's OVERLAY display list away from the world
list in VR gameplay, so the HUD can be rendered once into its own framebuffer
and composited as a flat plane in front of the player (VR-spec D7,
VR-DONOR-MAP 2 "why it's really an overlay routing switch" + 6).

THE BUG THIS FIXES, stated precisely. OoT's HUD, the scene title card
("Kokiri Forest"), dialogue boxes and the title/intro overlay are TEXRECT
draws: GfxDpTextureRectangle builds screen-space vertices directly and never
touches MP_matrix, so overlay 0031's projection injector cannot reach them and
0031's ortho gate deliberately leaves them on the game's own matrix. Both eyes
therefore receive the SAME PIXEL POSITIONS. On a headset with strongly
asymmetric per-eye frusta -- the device measures
eye0_tan = -1.7321,1.0000 and eye1_tan = -1.0000,1.7320 -- identical pixel
positions are NOT the same direction, so the HUD lands at two different world
directions and never fuses. It is also pushed toward the top and bottom edges,
which is why the user could "barely see" it (2026-09-03, build 1.0.1.2).

Vanilla chains the overlay onto the tail of the world list:

    gSPBranchList(POLY_XLU_DISP++, gfxCtx->overlayBuffer);

The donor's fix is not a new renderer feature, it is a ROUTING switch
(graph.c:307-347): in gameplay, terminate POLY_XLU properly and hand the
overlay buffer to the HUD pass instead; in a flat-screen context, keep the
vanilla chain, because there the overlay carries the ENTIRE interface -- the
kaleido inventory pages AND the pause background-capture command -- which must
land in the panel image as one picture. Routing that to the HUD plane would
put the inventory on a floating quad while the panel stayed black.

We reproduce the switch exactly, gated on the SAME latch overlay 0039 uses, so
the routing decision and the flat/panel decision can never disagree about a
frame. gSohVRHudPlane is the shell's A/B: 0 puts the HUD back on the world list
(1.0.1.2's behaviour, doubled) in one console command, which is how the fix
gets a red control in the headset.

The consumer is overlay 0031 rev15's third interpreter walk. __IOS__-only; the
oracle and every iPhone build emit the vanilla branch.

--- a/soh/src/code/graph.c
+++ b/soh/src/code/graph.c
@@ -310,7 +310,30 @@
 
     gSPBranchList(WORK_DISP++, gfxCtx->polyOpaBuffer);
     gSPBranchList(POLY_OPA_DISP++, gfxCtx->polyXluBuffer);
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    /* SOH_IOS (overlay 0043, VR-spec D7 / VR-DONOR-MAP 2+6): in VR gameplay
+     * the overlay buffer leaves the world list and becomes its own render
+     * pass, composited as a flat plane with real per-eye disparity. In a
+     * flat-screen context it stays chained -- there the overlay IS the whole
+     * interface and belongs in the panel image. See the patch header. */
+    {
+        extern volatile int gSohVRMode;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRFlatLatch;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRHudPlane;
+        extern void* volatile gSohVROverlayDL;
+        if ((gSohVRMode != 0) && (gSohVRFlatLatch == 0) && (gSohVRHudPlane != 0)) {
+            gDPPipeSync(POLY_XLU_DISP++);
+            gDPFullSync(POLY_XLU_DISP++);
+            gSPEndDisplayList(POLY_XLU_DISP++);
+            gSohVROverlayDL = (void*)gfxCtx->overlayBuffer;
+        } else {
+            gSPBranchList(POLY_XLU_DISP++, gfxCtx->overlayBuffer);
+            gSohVROverlayDL = NULL;
+        }
+    }
+#else
     gSPBranchList(POLY_XLU_DISP++, gfxCtx->overlayBuffer);
+#endif
     gDPPipeSync(OVERLAY_DISP++);
     gDPFullSync(OVERLAY_DISP++);
     gSPEndDisplayList(OVERLAY_DISP++);
